Offer a picture on the Android clipboard to X clients.

Upstream's clipboard bridge is text only: the server takes the CLIPBOARD selection when the host
announces a clip, lists STRING, TEXT and UTF8_STRING in TARGETS, and answers a request with the
text the host sends. A screenshot copied on the phone could not be pasted into an X program. The
Java side cannot do this alone: the selection owner is the server itself (lorie's
`lorieOwnSelection`), and only the owner can name targets and fill the requestor's property.
Running `xclip` inside the session as a second owner was the alternative, and would hold the
picture in a client for as long as it owned the selection.

So the owner learns picture targets. `lorie.h` gains three events sharing one payload struct:

- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_ANNOUNCE_TARGETS` (host to server): whether the clip has text, then the image
  targets one per line (the clip's own type and `image/png`, chosen by `LorieView`). The server
  interns them (at most `LORIE_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_TARGETS_MAX`) and takes the selection as the plain
  announce does. TARGETS lists the text targets only when the clip has text, then the image
  targets; a text request on a clip with no text is refused. Upstream's `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_ANNOUNCE`
  is unchanged and still means "text, no picture", so text paste is exactly as before.
- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_REQUEST` (server to host): an X client asked for an image target. The
  request is queued, and asked of the host once per target however many clients wait on it.
  `activity.cpp` calls `LorieView.requestClipboardImage(String)`, resolved in `nativeInit` with
  `FindMethodOrDie`.
- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_SEND` (host to server): the target answered, then the picture's bytes;
  no bytes is a refusal. Every request waiting on that target is answered from them — the
  property typed as the target, format 8 — and the bytes are freed at once: a picture is never
  cached, unlike upstream's text. Answers over `LORIE_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_MAX` (32 MiB) are read off
  the socket and dropped, and their requesters refused.

No INCR: INCR exists for an owner whose ChangeProperty would pass the maximum request size, and
this owner is the server, which writes the property directly; the requestor reads it with one
GetProperty, whose reply length has no such limit.

A requester must never be left waiting. Waiting requests are refused when the next announce
arrives, when the host disconnects, when there is no host to ask, and when the answer cannot be
written; a request whose client has gone in the meantime is dropped without writing to it.

The picture is the one payload larger than a socket buffer, so both ends move it with
read/write loops (`lorieReadFully`, `lorieWriteFully`) instead of upstream's single call. The host
writes it from `sendClipboardImage`, a JNI method registered here, in 16 KiB pieces copied out of
the Java array, on the main thread like every other write to the connection (the socket is one
stream; a second writer would interleave). `LorieView` must declare it as a plain native, not
`@FastNative`, since it can block.

Applies after 0001-0003; it extends the event enum, the `nativeInit` block and the event loops
those patches touch. `LorieView` must carry `@Keep requestClipboardImage(String)`,
`sendClipboardAnnounceTargets` and `sendClipboardImage`, or the process dies on the first view, so
the Java half ships in the same commit as this patch and the prebuilts built from it.

See x11-server/UPSTREAM.md.

